<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ACs B7.1 and B7.2 for proposal 36 — a CAPTURE-BACKED clip keeps its channels,
  and it keeps them all the way into the RENDERED FILE.

  Three clips, three windows, one render, and the same fixture in all three, so
  the only variable is which path the audio took:

    0.00-1.25 s   STRETCHED x1.25   AC B7.1   regression (B3 widened it)
    1.50-2.50 s   PITCHED +700c     AC B7.2a  regression (B3 widened it)
    3.00-4.00 s   CONTAINER/ASSET   AC B7.2b  NEW — this is what B7 built

  WHY THE FIRST TWO ARE REGRESSIONS AND ARE HERE ANYWAY. Proposal 36 §2 item 2
  was corrected by B3: SCut::rebuildReader calls buildCapture_ only when there is
  NO random source, so a sample-backed stretched or pitched clip builds
  twGrainSource straight over the file and reads it through a plain
  twSampleReader — which went wide at B3, with no capture on the playback path at
  all. Until B5 that could only be asserted AT A COMPONENT (assert-clip-channels,
  mc_stereo_clip_width). The sink is wide now, so it can be asserted in the file,
  which is a different claim: it says the width survived the track mix, the
  plugin chain, the rewire, the master sum and the interleave, not just the
  reader. That is worth a gate even though no B7 code produced it.

  THE THIRD IS THE MILESTONE. A container/asset clip is the one clip shape that
  really does go through SCut::buildCapture_ -> twCapturingSource, and that
  capture was built `twCapturingSource( ..., 1, ... )` — one channel, hard-coded,
  since proposal 07. It was the last mono page in the whole corpus (B3 measured
  it: "14.0 MiB with exactly one twSampleReader page still mono"). The capture
  now carries the container's declared width, so the reader over it is wide, so
  the ladder below survives instead of being duplicated by twTrackMix's clamp.

  THE FIXTURE, and why it can tell the two states apart (§7 trap 22). This case
  uses ../test_stereo.wav, NOT ../test_sawtooth.wav. The sawtooth's two channels
  are byte-identical, so a render made from it passes every channel assertion
  whether the signal path is wide or duplicating — that is exactly how
  channel_assert_dupmono turned out not to be the B5 gate it was designed as.
  test_stereo is gen_channel_fixture at channels=2: a 480 Hz sine at rms exactly
  0.5 on channel 0 and exactly 0.25 on channel 1 — a 6 dB ladder that a
  duplicating path CANNOT produce, because a duplicate is 0.5/0.5.

  MEASURED, on the build that introduced this case (all three windows):

      window      ch0 rms   ch1 rms   ratio
      stretched   0.4971    0.2486    2.000
      pitched     0.4956    0.2478    2.000
      asset       0.4999    0.2500    2.000

  and BEFORE the change, with buildCapture_ still hard-coding 1 channel, the
  asset window read 0.4999 / 0.4999 and the clip assertion reported 1 channel.
  The stretched and pitched windows were unchanged — which is the independent
  signal that what moved is the capture and not the width plumbing.

  THE CONTAINER TRACK IS MUTED, deliberately, and it still works: mute belongs to
  the mixer CHANNEL, not to the track (twtrackmix.h), so nobody sums track 2 into
  the master while SCut::buildCapture_ freezes its component directly and
  captures its material in full. That is what lets the asset have its own clean
  window instead of sounding on top of its own source.

  ASSET CLIP CHANNELS AT position 0 read 0.4279 / 0.2140, not 0.4999 / 0.2500,
  and that is right rather than a leak: the capture is 48000 frames and the page
  is 65536, so the trailing 17536 frames are silence and
  0.5 * sqrt(48000/65536) = 0.4279. The ladder is intact; the window is not full.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="mc_capture_clip_width">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- 0 stretched | 1 pitched | 2 the asset's container (muted) | 3 the asset -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- 1. STRETCHED x1.25. 48000 source frames out at 60000, so the vocoder
         really runs (a stretch of 1.0 would take the plain path). -->
    <place-clip trackPath="0" filePath="../test_stereo.wav"
                timePos="0" startOffset="0" duration="48000"/>
    <resize-clip clip="0,0" startTime="0" startOffset="0"
                 duration="60000" loopLength="0" stretch="1.25"/>

    <!-- 2. PITCHED +700 cents = a fifth up; pitch alone, no stretch, so the two
         vocoder paths are separable by window in the render. -->
    <place-clip trackPath="1" filePath="../test_stereo.wav"
                timePos="72000" startOffset="0" duration="48000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="1,0" cents="700"/>

    <!-- 3. THE CONTAINER, and its ASSET placed well clear of it. -->
    <place-clip trackPath="2" filePath="../test_stereo.wav"
                timePos="0" startOffset="0" duration="48000"/>
    <set-track-mute trackPath="2" muted="1"/>
    <create-asset container="2" startOffset="0" duration="48000"
                  assetName="mcCapAsset"/>
    <place-asset assetName="mcCapAsset" trackPath="3" timePos="144000"/>

    <!-- ============ at the CLIP, through the real scheduler ============= -->
    <!-- assert-clip-channels resolves through SCut::resolveClip and declares a
         demand on the project's CaptureRevalidator, so the page it reads was
         planned, dependency-counted and frozen on a worker exactly as playback
         readahead and the offline render do. Nothing downstream is in the way,
         which is what separates "the clip is wide" from "the file is wide". -->
    <assert-clip-channels clip="0,0" position="0" expectChannels="2"
                          channelA="0" channelB="1"
                          minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>
    <assert-clip-channels clip="1,0" position="0" expectChannels="2"
                          channelA="0" channelB="1"
                          minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>
    <!-- THE B7 ASSERTION. Before this milestone the capture was one channel
         wide and this line reported 1. -->
    <assert-clip-channels clip="3,0" position="0" expectChannels="2"
                          channelA="0" channelB="1"
                          minRmsDelta="0.18" minDiffRms="0.18"/>

    <!-- …and it must be able to fail, in both directions: a width of 1 is now
         wrong, and the ladder's 0.214 at this position is below 0.30. -->
    <assert-clip-channels clip="3,0" position="0" expectChannels="1"
                          channelA="0" channelB="1" minRmsDelta="0.0"
                          expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-channels clip="3,0" position="0" expectChannels="2"
                          channelA="0" channelB="1" minRmsDelta="0.30"
                          expectReject="true"/>

    <render filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>

    <!-- ================== AC B7.1 — in the FILE ======================== -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="4000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.45" maxRms="0.55"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="4000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.20" maxRms="0.30"/>
    <assert-channels-differ filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="4000" frameCount="48000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>

    <!-- ================== AC B7.2a — pitched, in the FILE ============== -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="76000" frameCount="40000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.45" maxRms="0.55"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="76000" frameCount="40000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.20" maxRms="0.30"/>
    <assert-channels-differ filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="76000" frameCount="40000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>

    <!-- ============ AC B7.2b — CONTAINER/ASSET, in the FILE ============ -->
    <!-- The new one. 0.4999 / 0.2500 where a one-channel capture gave
         0.4999 / 0.4999 and this assertion could not have been written. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="148000" frameCount="40000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.45" maxRms="0.55"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="148000" frameCount="40000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.20" maxRms="0.30"/>
    <assert-channels-differ filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="148000" frameCount="40000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>

    <!-- The band must be able to fail: the ladder gives delta 0.25 exactly. -->
    <assert-channels-differ filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="148000" frameCount="40000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.30"
                            expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- A third channel does not exist — an out-of-range channel is an ERROR,
         never a silent rms 0 (M0). -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_capture_clip_width.wav"
                         startFrame="148000" frameCount="40000"
                         channel="2" minRms="0.0" maxRms="1.0"
                         expectReject="true"/>

    <report-page-memory label="mc_capture_clip_width after render"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="4"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
